Title: "Type Resolution"
Id: "2b59cf98-6cf3-4b4b-835d-baabd8ee216b"

Text: "A binary operator resolves in this order:

1. application of a bound symbol (`x * y` becomes `*(x, y)`);
2. a colon-qualified capability owned by the selected operand type;
3. a matching `Disambiguates:` branch;
4. a capability owned by the operands' common type, after reducible spec facts
   are converted to type facts.

```mlg-fragment
x :* y
x *: y
x :*: y
```

These spellings select the left type, right type, or least common ancestor type.
Named operators use the same colon variants. Prefix, postfix, and member forms
follow analogous capability and disambiguation rules.

Capabilities from `Requires:` and `Enables:` are considered together, and
lookup follows `Declares:` extension chains. `from:` capabilities apply to a
compatible built literal. Assignment-backed `specifies:` facts are normalized
so capabilities stated in terms of a component can follow that component's
assigned command value."
Id: "b4f93e01-3b86-4228-b3ed-512add4b38bf"

SectionTitle: "Builds And Views"
Id: "88aed822-a5b4-4f56-8966-ee206c1c5290"

Text: "A build `\type@value` succeeds when the value already has the type, reaches it
through extension, or has an applicable `Enables: view:`.

```mlg-fragment
Enables:
. view:
  as: r := \as.rational{n} is \rational
  signifies: n \.embedded.to./ r
```

The view symbol, its value, and its type are available to `signifies:`. If the
view expression is a name or tuple of names, facts follow through those concrete
components; otherwise only the advertised type is known. Views satisfy resolved
command requirements and builds. A view can also supply the operand type needed
to resolve an infix specification operator. It does not select ordinary
overloaded operators or `Disambiguates:` branches.

Hard builds (`@!`), hard declarations (`is!`), `\\coercion`, `\\encoding`, and
the old `Enables: relation:` form are not supported."
Id: "40a048de-3573-4d73-ad8c-ca8c1772687d"
